---
/**
 * Home — the two-act landing. PUB-01, PUB-13, SEO-01. Plans 05-11, 05-16 and 05-17.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * THE MARKER. `home-render-ok` IS A BEHAVIOURAL CONTRACT AND IT IS UNCHANGED BY THIS REBUILD.
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * `GET /` returning a known marker is asserted by two suites and described in a third file:
 *
 *     test/harness/preview-reachable.node.test.ts   the built site is reachable over real workerd
 *     test/auth/deny-unauthenticated.node.test.ts   the AUTH-01/AUTH-04 suite — the middleware did
 *                                                   NOT run at build time and bake a 401 into the
 *                                                   public site's static HTML (threat T-02-35)
 *     src/middleware.ts                             states the contract in its own header
 *
 * The marker is **`home-render-ok`**, carried by `data-home-marker` on this page's `<h1>`.
 *
 * It is on the `<h1>` on purpose, and the `<h1>` is a design-system `Heading` — a React 19
 * component rendered with NO `client:*` directive. That preserves everything the old marker
 * proved, in one assertion: the build ran, a React component became static HTML with no hydration
 * script, the output was served by workerd, and the middleware let the prerendered route through.
 *
 * It is an ATTRIBUTE rather than the heading's text. The heading's text is `home_config.json`'s
 * `title`, which is reviewed content an editor may legitimately change; an attribute in code
 * cannot be edited out of the CMS by accident.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * 🔴 THE SOURCE OF TRUTH IS A FILE THAT RENDERS, AND THE LAST TWO ATTEMPTS DID NOT OPEN IT
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * `design_handoff_portfolio/Akhil Saxena - Home.dc.html` is a WORKING PROTOTYPE of the approved
 * design. 05-11 built this page from `05-UI-SPEC.md`; 05-16 found the spec had drifted and rebuilt
 * it from the LEGACY stylesheet, using the handoff for detail only. Akhil rejected both:
 * *"rudimentary… like something made by a ten year old."*
 *
 * This plan rendered the prototype in Chromium at 1280x860, measured it, and captured the result
 * beside it. Where `05-UI-SPEC.md` and the prototype disagree, **the prototype wins**, and every
 * divergence is named — here, in `src/styles/home.css`, and in the plan's report.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * THE FOUR COMPONENT CHOICES 05-16 GOT WRONG — ALL FOUR WERE ANSWERABLE FROM THE `.d.ts`
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * Recorded because the pattern matters more than the four instances: each was reported as "the
 * design system cannot express this", and in each case the component's own type declaration says
 * otherwise. Reading `dist/components/*.d.ts` before concluding a gap is the discipline.
 *
 *   1. `AppBar` for the top row. The design has NO bar. MEASURED, `primitives.css`:
 *      `.ds-atom-appbar` is `background: var(--surf-2); backdrop-filter: blur(14px)` and none of
 *      the four `AppBarVariant`s removes it. The row is two `Link`s and an `IconButton` in a flex
 *      row — see `src/components/public/PublicNav.tsx`.
 *
 *   2. `Heading` at the default 28px, with `HeadingSizeToken` stopping at `4xl` (44) reported as
 *      the blocker (D-7). MEASURED, `Heading.d.ts`: `size?: number | HeadingSizeToken`. The
 *      NUMERIC path was always there and always inlined the exact value — `size={60}` is the
 *      design's 60px, exactly, and it is what ships below. D-7 is a real gap in the TOKEN path and
 *      it was never a gap in the component.
 *
 *   3. `Text` + a `style={{ fontFamily: 'var(--display)' }}` escape hatch for the serif subtitle
 *      and intro, filed as D-22. MEASURED, `Heading.d.ts`: `as?: ElementType`, and
 *      `chunk-DQHLFJNO.js` inlines `fontFamily: "var(--display)"` unconditionally. **`Heading
 *      as="p"` IS serif body text**, through the component's own API, with no escape hatch at all.
 *      The three identity lines below use it and the hatch is gone from this page.
 *      D-22 stands as a finding about `Text` — a consumer that needs `Text`'s `leading` or
 *      `maxWidth` in the display face still cannot have it — but Home no longer depends on it.
 *
 *   4. `FilterNav` for the Photos filters (not this page, recorded for completeness): a segmented
 *      capsule where the design is an outlined pill. `Chip` is `border: 1px solid var(--rule)`.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * 🟠 THE ONE THING AKHIL ASKED FOR THAT THIS PLAN DOES NOT SHIP — READ BEFORE "FIXING" IT
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * *"I do not want the links to be that rudimentary… It should instead be buttons."*
 *
 * A button-shaped ANCHOR is not expressible in `2.0.0-beta.2`, and the two obvious ways round it
 * were both measured and both refused:
 *
 *   `Button` has no `as`.  `ButtonProps extends ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement>` and the
 *   prop list is `variant`, `size`, `loading`, `icon` — MEASURED, `Button.d.ts`. It is always a
 *   `<button>`, and a `<button>` inside an `<a>` is invalid HTML. Filed as D-5, and D-6 files the
 *   same asymmetry across four components. **beta.3 is adding it.**
 *
 *   AND ITS SHAPE CANNOT BE BORROWED, WHICH IS A NEW MEASUREMENT AND STRENGTHENS D-5.
 *   The obvious workaround — put `className="ds-atom-btn"` on a `Link` and let the design system's
 *   own stylesheet draw it — DOES NOT WORK. MEASURED, `chunk-UHK72XDD.js`: `baseStyle`,
 *   `sizeStyles` and `variantStyles` are INLINE objects on the element. `padding`, `border-radius`,
 *   `font-size`, `font-weight` and the fill all live in JavaScript, not in `primitives.css`, so an
 *   anchor carrying the class inherits a transition and a hover rule and nothing that makes it look
 *   like a button. There is no class-borrow path, so `as` is not one of several fixes — it is the
 *   only one.
 *
 *   `Chip` is not a substitute either. MEASURED, `chunk-JVIQWPC7.js`: `fontSize: 11`,
 *   `padding: "4px 10px"`, `borderRadius: 999` — a ~21px tag, not a control. Dressing a CTA as a
 *   tag would be a worse answer than a link.
 *
 * So the CTAs ship as `Link`, per this plan's brief, and the seam is marked below. **Hand-rolling
 * button CSS in `home.css` is the one thing that must not happen here** — the Core Value says a
 * gap the design system exposes is a FINDING, not a workaround, and `home.css` contains no
 * padding, border or radius for `.hm-cta` at all.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * ZERO FRAMEWORK JAVASCRIPT (PUB-14, §5.1 route 1)
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * No `client:*` directive appears on this page or in any component it renders, including the
 * `<h1>` that docks itself into the corner on scroll: that interaction is `animation-timeline:
 * scroll()` plus `position: sticky`, with no listener and no island. See `home.css` §4.
 */
import { Heading } from '@akhil-saxena/design-system/components/Heading';
import { Button } from '@akhil-saxena/design-system/components/Button';
import { Link } from '@akhil-saxena/design-system/components/Link';
/*
 * THE SCHEMA-VALIDATED EXPORT, NOT THE RAW JSON.
 *
 * This imported `data/home_config.json` directly, which typed `ctas` from the LITERAL. With
 * the array now empty that inferred `never[]`, and `cta.style` stopped existing as a property —
 * three type errors from a data edit that broke no code.
 *
 * `src/lib/content.ts` parses the same file through `HomeConfigSchema` at module scope and
 * exports it as `HomeConfig`, so the element type comes from the SCHEMA and does not move when
 * the data does. It also means a malformed record fails the build here, which the raw import
 * never did.
 */
import { home as homeConfig } from '../lib/content';
import PeekGrid from '../components/public/PeekGrid.astro';
import Seo from '../components/public/Seo.astro';
import PublicLayout from '../layouts/PublicLayout.astro';
import '../styles/home.css';

/**
 * The scroll cue, and **this string changed in 05-17**.
 *
 *     was   `SCROLL FOR THE WORK ↓`   pinned in test/public/home.node.test.ts, described in
 *                                      test/public/copy-contract.node.test.ts's header
 *     now   `↓ THE WORK`               the design handoff's own wording, verbatim
 *
 * 05-16 kept the pinned string over the handoff's, arguing that it "says what the control DOES
 * rather than where it points". Akhil's answer to the result was: *"The scroll for work should not
 * be this apparent. It should just be a small animation with an arrow showcasing that I need to
 * scroll, not this."* Four shouted words and an underline are the "this". The design's cue is two
 * words behind a leading arrow, mono, muted, breathing — and the arrow LEADS, which is why it is
 * `↓ THE WORK` and not `THE WORK ↓`.
 *
 * BOTH PINS WERE UPDATED IN THE SAME COMMIT AS THIS LINE. A copy contract whose pin is edited
 * without its subject is a contract; one edited with it is a note.
 */

/**
 * The behavioural marker `GET /` must carry. See this file's header, and `src/middleware.ts`,
 * which names the same string.
 */
const HOME_RENDER_MARKER = 'home-render-ok';

/**
 * The identity lines and the two CTAs, all four READ from `data/home_config.json` and none of
 * them written. Home's three strings are deliberately unpinned so Akhil can edit them through
 * `/admin`; `test/public/copy-contract.node.test.ts` asserts the page renders what the RECORD
 * says rather than a literal, so editing the record moves both sides together.
 */
const { title, subtitle, intro, description, ctas } = homeConfig;

/**
 * The `style` field on a CTA record is CONTENT, so it cannot be trusted to name a design-system
 * variant directly — a typo in the CMS would render an unstyled link with no error. It is mapped,
 * and an unmapped value THROWS at build time rather than shipping a bare anchor on the site's
 * primary route.
 *
 * `default` and `quiet` are the only two stylesheet-only `Link` variants. §4.6a MEASURED that
 * `inline`, `footer` and `action` set `color` — and `action` and `footer` also set
 * `textDecorationColor: "rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25)"` — as INLINE styles, which beat any app rule at any
 * specificity while every jsdom test still passes, because jsdom implements no CSS specificity.
 * On `#0d0d0f` that underline is invisible. Do not "improve" these to `action`; see D-4.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The CTAs are `Button as="a"` — a control that looks like a button and navigates like a link.
 *
 * That was not expressible until `2.0.0-beta.3`. `Button` had no `as`, and its shape could not be
 * borrowed either: `baseStyle`, `sizeStyles` and `variantStyles` are inline objects, so padding,
 * radius, type and fill live in JavaScript and `className="ds-atom-btn"` on a `Link` inherits a
 * transition and nothing else. `as` was the only fix, not one of several — which is why these
 * shipped as text links for two iterations and Akhil rejected both.
 *
 * beta.3 translates `disabled` rather than forwarding it (an anchor gets `aria-disabled`, loses its
 * `href` and its tab stop) and never emits `type` on a non-button. `.hm-cta` still carries no
 * padding, border or radius — the shape is the design system's, not ours.
 */
const CTA_VARIANT: Record<string, 'primary' | 'secondary'> = {
  primary: 'primary',
  secondary: 'secondary',
};

const homeCtas = ctas.map((cta) => {
  const variant = CTA_VARIANT[cta.style];
  if (!variant) {
    throw new Error(
      `Home: home_config.ctas has style "${cta.style}", which has no Link variant in CTA_VARIANT ` +
        `(${Object.keys(CTA_VARIANT).join(', ')}). Rendering an unstyled anchor would put an ` +
        "unthemed control in the middle of the site's first viewport with nothing to catch it.",
    );
  }
  return { ...cta, variant };
});
---

<PublicLayout mainClass="pub-max-home" navVariant="plain">
  <Seo slot="head" title={`${title} — ${subtitle}`} description={description} canonical="/" />

  {
    /*
    Act 1 is a plain <div>, not a <section>: an unnamed <section> is not exposed as a region, and a
    named one here would make FOUR landmarks where §6.6.4 specifies three. The gallery inside it
    carries `aria-label="Photographs"`.

    Nothing here is hidden from assistive technology. The name docking into the corner and the rest
    of the act fading out are a SCROLL POSITION and an OPACITY — `display: none`, `visibility:
    hidden`, `aria-hidden` and `inert` are all forbidden on both, and a screen-reader user reads
    name → subtitle → intro → photographs → work → résumé linearly and never encounters the
    interaction at all. A reader who has asked for reduced motion gets no interaction either, and
    an identical document.

    The `<h1>` is a DIRECT child of `.hm-a` and the rest is wrapped twice, and every level of that
    nesting is load-bearing rather than tidy:

      .hm-a      `position: sticky` resolves against the nearest block container, so the name needs
                 `.hm-a`'s full `100svh` of travel — inside a 90px identity block it would unpin
                 almost immediately.
      .hm-shed   ONE element carries the fade, so `.hm-cue` keeps its own `animation-name` — a
                 second one on the same element would replace it outright.
      .hm-lede   the subtitle and the intro fade FASTER than the photographs, and they have to:
                 MEASURED, the name pins at scrollY 55 and is still 48px at scrollY 120, by which
                 point the subtitle's flow position has slid to y=27.8 — behind the name. The two
                 sheds are the same mechanism at two rates, which needs two elements.

    See `home.css` §4.
  */
  }
  <div class="hm-a">
    {
      /*
        `size={60}` is the design's 60px through `Heading`'s NUMERIC path — see this file's header
        on D-7. It is kept even though `style` overrides the `fontSize` it inlines, because its
        OTHER inline is the size-derived `letterSpacing` (`--ls-tighter` above 28px), which is what
        the design asks for and which the token path does not emit.

        `style` spreads LAST in `Heading` (MEASURED, `chunk-DQHLFJNO.js`), which is the only way
        past the numeric path's inline `fontSize` — and it has to be got past, because the name
        steps down a rung below 673px and an inline style cannot be reached by a media query. Both
        values handed through are tokens; `--hm-name` is driven by `home.css` §2.
      */
    }
    <Heading
      level={1}
      size={48}
      weight={700}
      className="hm-name"
      style={{ fontSize: 'var(--hm-name)', lineHeight: 'var(--lh-tight)' }}
      data-home-marker={HOME_RENDER_MARKER}
    >
      {title}
    </Heading>

    <div class="hm-shed">
      {
        /*
          `Heading as="p"`, NOT `Text`. Both lines are Playfair in the design, and `Heading` inlines
          `fontFamily: var(--display)` unconditionally while `Text` inlines `var(--font)` with no
          lever to change it (D-22). `as` makes the ELEMENT a `<p>` while the VOICE stays the
          display face — which is the component's documented purpose: *"useful when the visual size
          and the semantic level diverge"*.

          The sizes are the design's, exactly: 20 and 21. `tone` rather than `color`, so the colour
          resolves from `[data-tone]` in the design system's stylesheet and the theme owns it.
          The italic is `home.css`'s, because `font-style` has no token and no component API.
        */
      }
      <div class="hm-lede">
        <Heading as="p" size={18} weight="regular" tone="muted" className="hm-subtitle">
          {subtitle}
        </Heading>

        {
          /* Rendered only when the record carries one — an empty `<p>` still takes its
            padding-block-start, so the gap under the subtitle would be wrong with nothing
            visible to explain it. The field survives so the admin can restore the line. */
        }
        {
          intro.length > 0 && (
            <Heading as="p" size={16} weight="regular" tone="secondary" className="hm-intro">
              {intro}
            </Heading>
          )
        }
      </div>

      <PeekGrid />

      {
        /*
        THE TWO DOORS — real `Button` components, not hand-rolled anchors.

        Act 1's job is a choice, not a scroll: a photographer takes one, a hiring manager takes the
        other, and neither is ranked above the other by word order or by weight. `primary` and
        `secondary` are the design system's own pair for exactly that — one filled, one outlined,
        equal size, equal position.

        `Button as="a"` because these navigate. It exists since 2.0.0-beta.3; before it, a
        button-shaped link was not expressible and this row would have been two text links.
      */
      }
      <div class="hm-doors">
        {
          /*
           * BOTH DOORS ARE OUTLINES, AND BOTH ARE `ghost`. Akhil: *"make both buttons same weight.
           * outline only ones. not filled. both are equal weight."*
           *
           * They were `primary` and `secondary`, which is a deliberate hierarchy — MEASURED, the
           * first rendered a solid #f2f2f4 fill with dark text and the second a #17171a panel, so
           * one door was plainly louder than the other. That was right when the page argued for
           * photography first; it is not what is wanted now.
           *
           * `ghost` IS THE ONLY VARIANT THAT CAN BE UNFILLED. `Button` composes
           * `{...baseStyle, ...sizeStyles, ...variantStyles[variant], ...style}` INLINE, so
           * `primary`'s and `secondary`'s backgrounds cannot be undone from `src/styles/` at any
           * specificity short of `!important`, which that tree forbids. `ghost` sets
           * `background: transparent`.
           *
           * THE `style` PROP IS THE DOOR THE COMPONENT LEAVES OPEN. `ghost` also sets
           * `borderColor: "transparent"` inline, so app CSS cannot reveal the edge either — but
           * `...style` is spread LAST, after the variant, so a border colour passed here wins by
           * the ordinary rules rather than by force. `.ds-atom-btn` already carries
           * `border: 1px solid var(--rule)`, so only the colour is needed.
           */
        }
        <Button
          as="a"
          href="/photography"
          variant="ghost"
          size="lg"
          className="hm-door"
          style={{ borderColor: 'var(--rule-strong)' }}
        >
          Photography →
        </Button>
        <Button
          as="a"
          href="/development"
          variant="ghost"
          size="lg"
          className="hm-door"
          style={{ borderColor: 'var(--rule-strong)' }}
        >
          Development →
        </Button>
      </div>

      {
        /*
            ACT 1 HAS NO CALLS TO ACTION, and `home_config.ctas` is empty — Akhil, 2026-08-30.

            The approved prototype's Act 1 carries none: the legacy site added them and the rebuild
            inherited them from there. The button was also the ONLY filled element on the page, so
            the eye reached it before the photographs, which is backwards for a photographer's
            landing. The order is now name -> photographs -> scroll.

            Nothing became unreachable. `/photography` is the nav's `photographs` AND the grid's own
            `ALL 40 →` badge, which links the whole block; `/development` is the nav and Act 2's
            `ALL WORK →`; `/resume` is Act 2's `RÉSUMÉ →`.

            The mapping and the render stay: the field is CMS content Akhil can refill, and an
            empty array must render nothing rather than an empty flex row that still takes its
            `gap` and its margins.
          */
      }
      {
        homeCtas.length > 0 && (
          <div class="hm-ctas">
            {homeCtas.map((cta) => (
              <Button as="a" href={cta.link} variant={cta.variant} size="lg" className="hm-cta">
                {cta.text}
              </Button>
            ))}
          </div>
        )
      }

      {
        /*
        NO SCROLL CUE. Act 1 is the whole page now — the two doors ARE the next step, so there is
        nothing below to point at. The cue's job was to say "there is more"; with Act 2 gone it
        would be pointing at the footer.
      */
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</PublicLayout>
